Step 1: Install Git

Go to the official website:

https://git-scm.com/downloads

Download the Windows version and install it.

During installation, you can keep the default settings.

Verify Installation

Open Command Prompt or Git Bash:

git --version

Example output:

git version 2.50.1

Step 2: Install VS Code

Download from:

https://code.visualstudio.com/

Install it with the default options.

Step 3: Install the GitHub Extension (Optional but Helpful)

Open VS Code.

Go to:

Extensions

Search:

GitHub Pull Requests

Install it.

This isn't required for basic Git, but it's useful later.

Step 4: Configure Git (Only Once)

Open Git Bash.

Run:

git config --global user.name "Geetha Priya"

Then:

git config --global user.email "YOUR_GITHUB_EMAIL"

Use the same email you used for your GitHub account.

Check the configuration:

git config --list

You should see:

user.name=Geetha Priya
user.email=your@email.com
Step 5: Clone Your Repository

Open GitHub.

Go to:

Data_Engineering_GP

Click:

Code

Copy the HTTPS URL.

It will look similar to:

https://github.com/geethapriyar30/Data_Engineering_GP.git

Open Git Bash:

Go to Documents:

cd Documents

Clone:

git clone https://github.com/geethapriyar30/Data_Engineering_GP.git

Git downloads your repository.

Step 6: Open the Repository in VS Code

Go inside:

cd Data_Engineering_GP

Open VS Code:

code .

If this doesn't work the first time, open VS Code manually and use:

File → Open Folder

Select:

Data_Engineering_GP
Step 7: Your Project in VS Code

You'll see something like:

📁 Data_Engineering_GP

    README.md

Now create folders:

01_SDLC

02_Agile

03_CI_CD

04_Jenkins_DevOps

05_Git_GitHub
Step 8: Create Your First File

Inside:

05_Git_GitHub

Create:

README.md

Write:

# Git and GitHub

## What is Git?

Git is a distributed version control system.

## What is GitHub?

GitHub hosts Git repositories online.

Save using:

Ctrl + S
Step 9: Check Git Status

Open the integrated terminal:

Terminal → New Terminal

Run:

git status

Example:

Untracked files:

05_Git_GitHub/

Git has detected your new folder.

Step 10: Stage the Changes
git add .

Check:

git status

Now you'll see:

Changes to be committed
Step 11: Commit
git commit -m "Added Git and GitHub notes"

Git saves a checkpoint.

Step 12: Push to GitHub
git push

Refresh GitHub.

🎉 Your new folder appears online.

How VS Code Helps

Look at the left sidebar.

You'll see the Source Control icon (looks like a branching graph).

When you modify a file:

README.md

VS Code marks it with:

M

(M = Modified)

Click Source Control.

You can:

View changed files
Stage changes
Enter a commit message
Commit
Sync/Push changes

You don't always need to type commands manually.